**The leak is at `loop.C:4`.** Each iteration does `new TH1F(...)` and never deletes it, so 10^6 histograms pile up on the heap.

There's a second cost on top of the leak. A `TH1F` constructed this way is registered in `gDirectory`, which is `gROOT` when no file is open. Even if you added `delete h`, ROOT would keep a name-to-object lookup for each histogram until it is deleted. Because the names are unique (`h0`, `h1`, ...), that lookup grows with every iteration. Each histogram is also small, but the per-object overhead (the name, title, and bin arrays) adds up to hundreds of MB over 10^6 events.

I haven't run the macro. This comes from reading the code.

**Fix**, depending on what you need:

1. **If you only need a scratch histogram per event**, create it on the stack and keep it out of the directory:
   ```cpp
   void loop() {
     TH1::AddDirectory(kFALSE);   // don't register histograms in gDirectory
     for (int i = 0; i < 1000000; ++i) {
       TH1F h("h", "", 10, 0, 1); // freed at end of each iteration
       h.Fill(0.5);
     }
   }
   ```
2. **Better, if the histogram is really one accumulator**, create it once before the loop and fill it inside:
   ```cpp
   TH1F* h = new TH1F("h", "", 10, 0, 1);
   for (...) h->Fill(x);
   ```
3. **If you must use `new` inside the loop**, call `delete h;` at the end of each iteration. Add `h->SetDirectory(nullptr)` first if a directory or file is open.

To confirm the fix, compare peak memory before and after, for example with `/usr/bin/time -v root -l -b -q loop.C`. You can also use `valgrind --leak-check=full` on a smaller loop count.

Your real macro is probably more complex than this fixture. If the leak persists after this change, send me the actual file. Other usual suspects are `TTree` or `TObject` allocations inside the loop, `TCanvas` objects, and `TFile`s that are opened but never closed.

